## **Psalm 5: The Ethics of Sincerity and Moral Exclusion**

### **Introduction to Psalm 5**

Psalm 5 is a morning prayer of David, contrasting the righteous who approach God with reverence against the wicked who are characterized by arrogance, falsehood, and violence. The psalmist petitions for guidance and protection, while pronouncing judgment upon those who "speak falsehood" and whose "interior is destruction."

Unlike Psalm 4 (response to slander and inner peace), Psalm 5 addresses the ethical themes of ****sincerity versus hypocrisy****, ****the fate of the wicked****, and ****the call for divine justice****. It also includes a striking affirmation: the righteous will be blessed and protected, while the wicked will be excluded from the divine presence.

### **Full Biblical Text**

The text of Psalm 5, as presented in the Bible (NRSVue), is as follows:

****To the leader: for the flutes. A Psalm of David.****

****1**** Give ear to my words, O LORD; give heed to my sighing. ****2**** Listen to the sound of my cry, my King and my God, for to you I pray. ****3**** O LORD, in the morning you hear my voice; in the morning I plead my case to you, and watch. ****4**** For you are not a God who delights in wickedness; evil will not sojourn with you. ****5**** The boastful will not stand before your eyes; you hate all evildoers. ****6**** You destroy those who speak lies; the LORD abhors the bloodthirsty and deceitful. ****7**** But I, through the abundance of your steadfast love, will enter your house; I will bow down toward your holy temple in awe of you. ****8**** Lead me, O LORD, in your righteousness because of my enemies; make your way straight before me. ****9**** For there is no truth in their mouths; their interior is destruction; their throat is an open grave; they flatter with their tongues. ****10**** Make them bear their guilt, O God; let them fall by their own counsels because of their many transgressions, for they have rebelled against you. ****11**** But let all who take refuge in you rejoice; let them ever sing for joy. Spread your protection over them, so that those who love your name may exult in you. ****12**** For you bless the righteous, O LORD; you cover them with favor as with a shield.

### **Extraction of Core Ethical Lessons**

The psalm presents a stark contrast between the righteous (who approach with sincerity and seek guidance) and the wicked (who are boastful, deceitful, bloodthirsty, and corrupt). The ethical content can be reduced to three distinct principles:

| **Principle**                                                     | **Ethical Core (Plain English)**                                                                                                                                                                                 |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| ****Principle 1: Sincerity in Moral Practice****                  | Moral integrity requires authenticity. The moral order cannot be deceived by outward displays of piety or flattering speech. True moral standing is based on internal character, not external performance.       |
| ****Principle 2: The Moral Exclusion of the Wicked****            | Those who are arrogant, deceitful, violent, and corrupt are incompatible with a just society. They are excluded from the blessings of the moral order and ultimately face the consequences of their own actions. |
| ****Principle 3: The Righteous Find Refuge in the Moral Order**** | Those who act with sincerity and integrity find protection, joy, and flourishing. The moral order is a refuge for the just.                                                                                      |

For our comparative purposes, ****Principle 1**** and ****Principle 2**** represent the most universally actionable ethical heuristics.

