## **Psalm 17: The Ethics of Maintaining Integrity Under Attack**

### **Introduction**

Psalm 17 is a prayer for deliverance and vindication, attributed to David. The psalmist protests his innocence, declaring that he has not spoken with deceitful lips or harboured violence in his heart. He calls upon the moral order to test him and to vindicate him against his enemies, who are described as arrogant and merciless. The psalm concludes with a confident declaration that the psalmist will behold the face of the moral order in righteousness and be satisfied.

Unlike Psalm 16 (contentment and trust), Psalm 17 addresses the ethical themes of ****innocence vindicated against false accusation****, ****protection from the arrogant and violent****, and ****the longing for ultimate justice****.

### **Full Biblical Text**

****A Prayer of David.****

****1**** Hear a just cause, O LORD; attend to my cry! Give ear to my prayer from lips free of deceit! ****2**** From you let my vindication come; let your eyes see the right. ****3**** If you try my heart, if you visit me by night, if you test me, you will find no wickedness in me; my mouth does not transgress. ****4**** As for what others do, by the word of your lips I have avoided the ways of the violent. ****5**** My steps have held fast to your paths; my feet have not slipped. ****6**** I call upon you, for you will answer me, O God; incline your ear to me; hear my words. ****7**** Wondrously show your steadfast love, O saviour of those who seek refuge from their adversaries at your right hand. ****8**** Guard me as the apple of the eye; hide me in the shadow of your wings, ****9**** from the wicked who despoil me, my deadly enemies who surround me. ****10**** They close their hearts to pity; their mouths speak with arrogance. ****11**** They track me down; now they surround me; they set their eyes to cast me to the ground. ****12**** They are like a lion eager to tear, like a young lion lurking in ambush. ****13**** Rise up, O LORD, confront them, overthrow them! By your sword deliver my life from the wicked, ****14**** from mortals—by your hand, O LORD— from mortals whose portion in life is in this world. May their bellies be filled with what you have stored up; may their children have more than enough; may they leave something over to their little ones. ****15**** As for me, I shall behold your face in righteousness; when I awake I shall be satisfied, beholding your likeness.

