## **Psalm 21: The Ethics of Gratitude and Acknowledging the Source of Success**

### **Introduction**

Psalm 21 is a royal thanksgiving psalm, celebrating the king's victory and the blessings bestowed upon him by the moral order. It is a companion to Psalm 20, which was a prayer for victory before battle; Psalm 21 is the praise offered after victory has been granted. The psalmist extols the king's strength, joy, and honour, which come not from his own might but from the favour of the moral order. The psalm concludes with a declaration that the moral order will destroy the wicked who plot against the king.

Unlike Psalm 20 (trust in the moral order versus military might, and communal intercession), Psalm 21 addresses the ethical themes of ****gratitude for deliverance****, ****the recognition that all success comes from the moral order****, and ****the eventual defeat of those who plot evil****.

### **Full Biblical Text**

****To the leader. A Psalm of David.****

****1**** In your strength the king rejoices, O LORD, and in your help how greatly he exults! ****2**** You have given him his heart's desire, and have not withheld the request of his lips. Selah ****3**** For you meet him with rich blessings; you set a crown of fine gold on his head. ****4**** He asked you for life; you gave it to him— length of days forever and ever. ****5**** His glory is great through your help; you bestow on him splendor and majesty. ****6**** You bestow on him blessings forever; you make him glad with the joy of your presence. ****7**** For the king trusts in the LORD, and through the steadfast love of the Most High he shall not be moved. ****8**** Your hand will find out all your enemies; your right hand will find out those who hate you. ****9**** You will make them like a fiery furnace when you appear. The LORD will consume them in his wrath, and fire will devour them. ****10**** You will destroy their offspring from the earth, and their children from among humankind. ****11**** If they plan evil against you, if they devise mischief, they will not succeed. ****12**** For you will put them to flight; you will aim your arrows at their faces. ****13**** Be exalted, O LORD, in your strength! We will sing and praise your power.

