Psalm 41 is the final psalm of Book I of the Psalter (Psalms 1–41). It is a psalm of lament and trust, attributed to David, that opens with a beatitude: "Happy are those who consider the poor; the LORD delivers them in the day of trouble." The psalmist reflects on the blessing that comes to those who care for the vulnerable, and then describes their own experience of betrayal and illness. The psalm concludes with a prayer for healing and vindication, and a confident declaration that the moral order will not abandon the faithful.

