****Second, self-encouragement in times of despair is a spiritual discipline.**** Whether framed as the psalmist's refrain, "Why are you cast down, O my soul? Hope in God" (Psalm 42), as the Jewish teaching that self-encouragement is modelled in scripture, as the Buddhist teaching of diligence and self-rousing, as the Gita's teaching that one should lift oneself up by one's own self, as the Qur'anic teaching not to weaken or grieve, as the Sikh teaching that the devotee reminds their soul of the Divine's presence, as the Confucian teaching that the noble person remains composed and encourages themselves, or as the African teaching that one must speak to one's own heart and remember the ancestors, all traditions teach that ****the faithful person must actively encourage themselves when they are in despair****. Hope is not passive; it is a discipline that requires intentional self-encouragement.

