This is ethically significant for our hypothesis. Religions that acknowledge the reality of injustice and encourage prayer for vindication provide a moral framework that sustains hope and prevents despair in the face of oppression. Societies that internalise these principles may be more stable, as individuals are less likely to take justice into their own hands through vigilantism or violence, and more likely to trust in the moral order's ultimate justice. The endurance of these traditions may therefore be partially explained by their capacity to instil ****hope and trust in justice****—qualities that favour long-term social stability.

### **Conclusion**

Psalm 43, Principle 1 ("The experience of injustice and oppression") and Principle 2 ("The prayer for vindication") find clear ethical equivalents in all seven other religions examined. The principles are expressed through different cultural lenses: as the prayer against an ungodly people (Psalm), as Job's suffering (Judaism), as the teaching that the good may suffer (Buddhism), as Yudhishthira's exile (Hinduism), as the Qur'anic teaching that the faithful will be tested, as the Sikh teaching that suffering is part of life, as the Confucian recognition that the noble person may be poor and unrecognized, and as the African understanding that the righteous may suffer but are not forgotten.

This convergence supports our central hypothesis: ****the world's most enduring religions contain a shared ethical core concerning the reality of injustice and the legitimacy of praying for vindication.**** The ethical lesson—that the faithful may suffer injustice, but that it is legitimate to pray for vindication and trust in the moral order's ultimate justice—transcends its religious packaging.

## **Psalm 44: The Ethics of Collective Suffering and Maintaining Integrity**

### **Introduction**

Psalm 44 is a communal lament of profound distress. The psalmist recalls the mighty deeds of the moral order in the past, when the ancestors were given victory over their enemies. But now, the community is suffering defeat, humiliation, and oppression, even though they have not forgotten or betrayed the moral order. The psalmist asks why the moral order has hidden its face and allowed them to be scattered among the nations. The psalm concludes with a desperate plea for deliverance.

### **Full Biblical Text**

****To the leader. Of the Korahites. A Maskil.****

****1**** We have heard with our ears, O God, our ancestors have told us, what deeds you performed in their days, in the days of old: ****2**** you with your own hand drove out the nations, but them you planted; you afflicted the peoples, but them you set free; ****3**** for not by their own sword did they win the land, nor did their own arm give them victory; but your right hand, and your arm, and the light of your countenance, for you delighted in them. ****4**** You are my King and my God; you command victories for Jacob. ****5**** Through you we push down our foes; through your name we tread down our assailants. ****6**** For not in my bow do I trust, nor can my sword save me. ****7**** But you have saved us from our foes, and have put to confusion those who hate us. ****8**** In God we have boasted continually, and we will give thanks to your name forever. Selah ****9**** Yet you have rejected us and abased us, and have not gone out with our armies. ****10**** You made us turn back from the foe, and our enemies have gotten spoil. ****11**** You have made us like sheep for slaughter, and have scattered us among the nations. ****12**** You have sold your people for a trifle, and demanded no high price for them. ****13**** You have made us the taunt of our neighbors, the derision and scorn of those around us. ****14**** You have made us a byword among the nations, a laughingstock among the peoples. ****15**** All day long my disgrace is before me, and shame has covered my face ****16**** at the words of the taunters and revilers, at the sight of the enemy and the avenger. ****17**** All this has come upon us, yet we have not forgotten you, or been false to your covenant. ****18**** Our hearts have not turned back, nor have our steps departed from your way, ****19**** yet you have broken us in the haunt of jackals, and covered us with deep darkness. ****20**** If we had forgotten the name of our God, or spread out our hands to a strange god, ****21**** would not God discover this? For he knows the secrets of the heart. ****22**** Because of you we are being killed all day long, and accounted as sheep for the slaughter. ****23**** Rouse yourself! Why do you sleep, O Lord? Awake, do not cast us off forever! ****24**** Why do you hide your face? Why do you forget our affliction and oppression? ****25**** For we sink down to the dust; our bodies cling to the ground. ****26**** Rise up, come to our help. Redeem us for the sake of your steadfast love.

